.\" scanf.3 - scanf library function family
.TH SCANF 3 "March 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
scanf, fscanf, sscanf, vscanf, vfscanf, vsscanf \- input format conversion
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.B #include <stdarg.h>
.PP
.BI "int scanf(const char *" format ", ...);"
.PP
.BI "int fscanf(FILE *" stream ", const char *" format ", ...);"
.PP
.BI "int sscanf(const char *" str ", const char *" format ", ...);"
.PP
.BI "int vscanf(const char *" format ", va_list " ap ");"
.PP
.BI "int vfscanf(FILE *" stream ", const char *" format ", va_list " ap ");"
.PP
.BI "int vsscanf(const char *" str ", const char *" format ", va_list " ap ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR scanf ()
family of functions scans input according to a
.I format
as described below.
This format may contain
.I "conversion specifiers" ;
the results from such conversions, if any, are stored in the locations pointed to by the
.I "pointer arguments"
that follow
.I format .
.PP
.BR fscanf ()
reads input from the stream pointer
.IR stream .
.PP
.BR sscanf ()
reads its input from the character string pointed to by
.IR str .
.PP
.BR vscanf (),
.BR vfscanf (),
and
.BR vsscanf ()
are equivalent to
.BR scanf (),
.BR fscanf (),
and
.BR sscanf ()
respectively, except that they are called with a
.I va_list
instead of a variable number of arguments.
.SH CONVERSIONS
The format string consists of a sequence of directives which describe how to process the sequence of input characters.
If a directive fails, no further input is read, and
.BR scanf ()
returns.
A directive is one of the following:
.IP \(bu 3
A sequence of white-space characters (space, tab, newline, etc.; see
.BR isspace (3)).
This directive matches any amount of white space, including none, in the input.
.IP \(bu 3
An ordinary character (i.e., one other than white space or '%').
This character must exactly match the next character of input.
.IP \(bu 3
A conversion specification, which commences with a '%' (percent) character.
A sequence of characters from the input is converted according to this specification, and the result is placed in the corresponding pointer argument.
.PP
Conversion specifications follow this format:
.PP
.RS 4
%[*] [width] [length] type
.RE
.PP
The optional '*' character indicates assignment suppression: the input is consumed but not stored.
The optional decimal integer
.I width
specifies the maximum number of characters to be read for this conversion.
The
.I length
modifiers (e.g.,
.BR l ,
.BR ll ,
.BR h ,
.BR hh )
specify the size of the destination.
.SH RETURN VALUE
These functions return the number of input items successfully matched and assigned, which can be fewer than provided for, or even zero in the event of an early matching failure.
The value
.B EOF
is returned if the end of input is reached before the first conversion or matching failure occurred.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR printf (3),
.BR strtol (3),
.BR strtod (3),
.BR isspace (3)
